# Rover Tracking Antenna V3.0 — Professional Codebase Review

> Reviewed as a robotics systems engineer. All 8 source files examined.

---

## 1. System Architecture Overview

```mermaid
graph LR
    subgraph Rover["🤖 Rover"]
        ROS2[ROS2 /nav_sat Topic]
    end
    subgraph Bridge["🖥️ PC Bridge"]
        PY[send_rover_coordinate.py<br/>ROS2 Node]
    end
    subgraph Base["📡 Base Station ESP32"]
        BS[baseStation.ino<br/>Serial Relay]
    end
    subgraph Antenna["🎯 Antenna ESP32"]
        HW[hardware_interface.cpp]
        TM[tracking_math.cpp]
        MAIN[antenna.ino]
        LW[Last_Wish.h]
    end

    ROS2 -->|NavSatFix| PY
    PY -->|USB Serial 115200| BS
    BS -->|Telemetry 9600| HW
    HW --> MAIN
    TM --> MAIN
    MAIN -->|Servo PWM| Servo[Servo Motor]
```

The system is a **3-node distributed tracking pipeline**:

| Node | Platform | Role |
|------|----------|------|
| **Bridge** | PC (ROS2) | Subscribes to rover GPS topic, forwards to base via USB serial |
| **Base Station** | ESP32 | Receives coordinates from PC, relays over 9600 baud telemetry to antenna |
| **Antenna** | ESP32 | Reads own GPS + compass + rover telemetry, computes bearing, drives servo |

**Data protocol** across all links: `lat:<value>,lon:<value>\n` — simple, consistent, human-readable.

---

## 2. File-by-File Analysis

---

### 2.1 `antenna/antenna.ino` — Main Control Loop
[antenna.ino](file:///Users/mdabiralsaba/Documents/Arduino/RoverTrackingAntenna%20V3.0/antenna/antenna.ino)

**83 lines. Clean, minimal, well-structured.**

The loop runs at **~10 Hz** (`delay(100)`) and follows a textbook control pipeline:

```
Read Sensors → Compute Bearing → Compute Error → Deadband → LPF → Servo Output
```

**Observations:**

- ✅ Clear 6-step pipeline with comments
- ✅ Servo angle clamped to [0°, 180°] before output
- ✅ 1 Hz serial diagnostics (avoids flooding)
- ✅ Validity check on coordinates before printing bearing/error

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **The LPF is applied to the heading error, not to the servo angle.** This is the correct approach — it smooths the control signal before it becomes a position command, preventing the filter from fighting the plant.

> [!WARNING]
> **`prev_error` is a global but used as LPF state.** If `antenna.ino` is ever compiled as part of a multi-file project with other globals named `prev_error`, there will be a linker collision. Should be `static`.

---

### 2.2 `antenna/tracking_math.h` / `tracking_math.cpp` — Navigation Mathematics
[tracking_math.cpp](file:///Users/mdabiralsaba/Documents/Arduino/RoverTrackingAntenna%20V3.0/antenna/tracking_math.cpp) · [tracking_math.h](file:///Users/mdabiralsaba/Documents/Arduino/RoverTrackingAntenna%20V3.0/antenna/tracking_math.h)

**80 lines. Purely mathematical — zero hardware dependencies. Fully portable.**

| Function | Purpose | Correctness |
|----------|---------|-------------|
| `compute_bearing()` | Great-circle initial bearing (Haversine forward azimuth) | ✅ Correct |
| `compute_distance()` | Haversine distance in meters | ✅ Correct |
| `normalize_angle()` | Wraps to [-180°, +180°] | ✅ Correct |
| `compute_heading_error()` | `normalize(bearing - heading)` | ✅ Correct |
| `low_pass_filter()` | First-order IIR: `α·current + (1-α)·previous` | ✅ Correct |
| `apply_deadband()` | Symmetric deadband | ✅ Correct |
| `compute_dynamic_deadband()` | Scales deadband [1°, 4°] proportional to distance | ✅ Correct |

**Observations:**

- ✅ All math is correct and uses standard geodesy formulas
- ✅ Clean separation of concerns — no Arduino/ESP32 includes
- ✅ `DEG_TO_RAD`, `RAD_TO_DEG`, `EARTH_RADIUS` defined as macros in the header

> [!NOTE]
> **Dynamic deadband design:** At close range (< 100m), deadband is 1°. At long range (> 400m), it maxes at 4°. The `scale = 0.01` factor means `deadband = distance_m × 0.01`. This is a good heuristic — at long range the rover subtends fewer degrees of arc, so oscillation from GPS noise is more problematic and a wider deadband prevents jitter.

> [!TIP]
> **LPF alpha = 0.2** (set in `antenna.ino`) at 10 Hz gives a **time constant of ~0.4 seconds**. This is fairly aggressive smoothing. Good for jitter suppression, but introduces ~400ms of tracking lag. This is acceptable for a ground rover (slow angular velocity relative to antenna), but would be too sluggish for a drone.

---

### 2.3 `antenna/hardware_interface.h` / `hardware_interface.cpp` — Hardware Abstraction Layer
[hardware_interface.cpp](file:///Users/mdabiralsaba/Documents/Arduino/RoverTrackingAntenna%20V3.0/antenna/hardware_interface.cpp) · [hardware_interface.h](file:///Users/mdabiralsaba/Documents/Arduino/RoverTrackingAntenna%20V3.0/antenna/hardware_interface.h)

**351 lines. The largest and most complex file. Handles 4 peripherals.**

#### Peripherals managed:

| Peripheral | Interface | Pins | Baud/Freq |
|------------|-----------|------|-----------|
| GPS (antenna's own) | UART1 | TX→GPIO4, RX→GPIO5 | 9600 |
| Telemetry (rover coords) | UART2 | RX=D16, TX=D17 | 9600 |
| Compass (HMC5883L) | I2C | SDA=21, SCL=22 | — |
| Servo | LEDC PWM | GPIO 14 | 50 Hz, 16-bit |

#### Initialization Sequence (`hardware_init()`):

1. GPS serial begin
2. Telemetry serial begin
3. Compass I2C init (8-average, 15 Hz, 1090 LSb/Gauss, continuous mode)
4. Servo LEDC setup (with ESP32 Arduino v3.x compatibility)
5. NVS restore last servo angle
6. Smooth sweep from saved → 90° home position

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **The startup sweep is excellent engineering.** On power-up, the servo doesn't jump to 90° — it slowly sweeps from its last-saved position at 0.5°/step with 15ms delay. This prevents mechanical shock and gear stripping. Total sweep time for worst case (0°→90°) = 180 steps × 15ms = **2.7 seconds**.

#### Servo Control:

```
angle → angle_to_pulse() → pulse_to_duty() → ledcWrite()
```

- Pulse range: **801–1641 µs** (calibrated to specific servo)
- 16-bit PWM at 50 Hz → resolution = 20000µs / 65535 = **0.305 µs per step** (excellent)
- NVS save is **debounced** — only writes to flash after 3 seconds of stability (prevents flash wear)

> [!WARNING]
> **ESP32 Arduino v3.x compatibility is properly handled** via `#if defined(ESP_ARDUINO_VERSION_MAJOR)` guards. Both `ledcAttach()` (v3+) and `ledcSetup()`/`ledcAttachPin()` (v2) paths are present. Good.

#### Compass Reading (`get_antenna_heading()`):

- Reads 6 bytes from HMC5883L register 0x03
- Byte order: X_high, X_low, Z_high, Z_low, Y_high, Y_low (correct for HMC5883L)
- Applies hard-iron calibration offsets and soft-iron scale factors
- Adds magnetic declination (-0.0089 rad ≈ -0.51°) and mounting offset (-88°)
- Normalizes to [0°, 360°)

> [!NOTE]
> **Mounting offset of -88°** means the compass module is physically rotated ~90° from the antenna's forward direction. This is calibrated for the physical build.

> [!CAUTION]
> **Critical issue:** If `Wire.available()` returns anything other than 6 (I2C failure, bus hang, sensor disconnected), `headingDegrees` stays at **0.0** and is returned silently. This means:
> - The servo will compute `error = bearing - 0.0 = bearing` and snap to whatever bearing is
> - There's **no error reporting** to the serial monitor
> - The system will behave as if the compass always reads North
> This is a **silent failure mode** that would cause erratic tracking.

#### Telemetry Parsing:

- Character-by-character buffering with `\n`/`\r` as delimiters
- 100-char buffer overflow protection
- Robust parser: checks for `lat:`, `lon:`, comma, validates range [-90,90] / [-180,180], rejects (0,0)
- Raw byte echo to Serial Monitor (useful for debugging baud mismatch)

